//? Title: Elastic Net: Thresholding, Grouping and Cross-Validation
//? Subtitle: Exact coordinate updates, fold-local preprocessing and Iris prediction
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Predicting a continuous response from correlated measurements can produce unstable
least-squares coefficients. We want to control coefficient size and possible sparsity
while measuring prediction error without fitting preprocessing on validation rows.
Elastic net combines L1 thresholding with quadratic shrinkage. Four-row examples
expose its exact coordinate solution, sensitivity to measurement units and grouping
of duplicated predictors. A small fold-refit check verifies the validation pipeline.
Iris then supplies coefficient paths, complete CV diagnostics and a predeclared
120/30-row training/test comparison of ridge, lasso, mixtures and unpenalized regression.
Numerical convergence, training fit, CV selection and held-out performance are reported
separately. The primary fit does not improve held-out RMSE over unpenalized regression
on this split; neither sparsity nor shrinkage alone certifies a useful predictor.
)
\toc

> The method

>> Specify the objective in the coordinates that are penalized

Let the expanded formula-design columns be x_j. With an intercept, fit training
means mu_j and response mean y_bar; without an intercept, both origins are zero.
Optional scaling uses s_j=sqrt(sum_i(x_ij−mu_j)^2/n), with an exactly zero divisor
replaced by one. Define Z_ij=(x_ij−mu_j)/s_j and u_i=y_i−y_bar. The model minimizes

$$
 J(\theta)=\frac{1}{2n}\|u-Z\theta\|_2^2+
 \lambda\left[\alpha\|\theta\|_1+\frac{1-\alpha}{2}\|\theta\|_2^2\right],
 \quad\beta_j=\theta_j/s_j,\quad
 b_0=\bar y-\sum_j\mu_j\beta_j.
$$
\alttext(The penalized objective is average squared error divided by two plus mixed L1 and squared L2 penalties on scaled slopes. Dividing by training scales restores original-unit slopes; the intercept reverses the fitted offsets.)

Here lambda>=0 controls strength and alpha in [0,1] controls the L1 mixture. Alpha=0
is `Ridge`, alpha=1 is `Lasso`; the wrappers use the same engine. The intercept is
unpenalized, and the response is centred when appropriate but never scaled. Formula
interactions are expanded first and then centred/scaled as their own design columns.
Reported coefficients are in original design units, so their penalty is not generally
lambda times their unscaled norm. `coefficient_table()` shows both coordinate systems.
Population RMS uses n, not n−1; without an intercept it is RMS about zero.

This is the direct penalized objective used in the glmnet coordinate-descent formulation.
It does not apply the additional coefficient rescaling of the original 2005 elastic-net
paper. Scikit-learn names the same parameters differently: its `alpha` is this lambda,
and its `l1_ratio` is this alpha. Standardize explicitly before comparing solvers.

The TikZ illustration shows a fixed penalty budget in two coefficient coordinates
and the thresholding operation. The curved contours do not by themselves establish
which coefficients a particular dataset will select. Regeneration requires LaTeX,
PGF/TikZ, standalone, Latin Modern and dvisvgm; exports use the saved SVG.

```{bash, results="output", file="figures/elastic_net_geometry.svg"}
set -eu
mkdir -p build
latex -interaction=batchmode -halt-on-error -output-directory=build diagrams/elastic_net_geometry.tikz > build/elastic_net_geometry.compile.log 2>&1
dvisvgm --no-fonts --bbox=10 --output=build/elastic_net_geometry.svg build/elastic_net_geometry.dvi > build/elastic_net_geometry.render.log 2>&1
printf 'Rendered the local penalty and threshold diagram.\n'
```
// result_begin:
// Rendered the local penalty and threshold diagram.
// \image(figures/elastic_net_geometry.svg)
// result_end
\caption(L1 contributes corners and a zero region; L2 supplies additional curvature in coefficient space.)
\alttext(Three contours show equal penalty budget one for ridge, a half-and-half elastic net and lasso. A second panel shows soft thresholding with a flat zero region between minus one half and one half and the full coordinate-update formula.)

>> Derive the exact coordinate minimizer

Hold every coefficient except theta_j fixed and put its old contribution back into
the residual. The one-dimensional objective has curvature q_j, not automatically one:

$$
 \rho_j=\frac1n Z_j^T\!\left(u-\sum_{\ell\ne j}Z_\ell\theta_\ell\right),\quad
 q_j=\frac1n\|Z_j\|_2^2,\qquad
 \theta_j\leftarrow\frac{S(\rho_j,\lambda\alpha)}{q_j+\lambda(1-\alpha)},\quad
 S(a,t)=\operatorname{sign}(a)\max(|a|-t,0).
$$
\alttext(Add back the old coordinate to form a partial residual. Its correlation with the predictor is soft-thresholded and divided by actual predictor squared norm plus the quadratic penalty.)

Differentiating on the positive and negative half-lines gives the two nonzero cases;
the subgradient interval at zero supplies the threshold region. For a zero column and
zero denominator, choose coefficient zero. Even when standardization is disabled,
this actual q_j is essential. Every exact coordinate update weakly decreases the
convex objective, subject to floating-point arithmetic. Positive lambda*(1−alpha)
makes the slope objective strictly convex; lasso and unpenalized least squares can have
nonunique slopes when the design is dependent.

For ridge, stationarity also gives

$$
 (Z^TZ+n\lambda I)\theta=Z^Tu,\qquad
 \kappa=\frac{d_{\max}+n\lambda}{d_{\min}+n\lambda}.
$$
\alttext(Ridge solves penalized normal equations. Its eigenvalue condition number is the largest shifted Gram eigenvalue divided by the smallest shifted eigenvalue.)

A positive lambda makes this matrix invertible, but an arbitrarily small positive
lambda does not guarantee a small condition number. Ridge does not impose a sparsity
threshold; nevertheless, a coefficient can be exactly zero for an irrelevant orthogonal
or constant column. The old report's “never zero” and unconditional conditioning claims
are therefore too strong.

>> Check stationarity as well as coefficient movement

Let c_j=Z_j^T(u−Z theta)/n−lambda*(1−alpha)*theta_j. Convex optimality requires

$$
 c_j=\lambda\alpha\operatorname{sign}(\theta_j)\quad(\theta_j\ne0),\qquad
 |c_j|\le\lambda\alpha\quad(\theta_j=0).
$$
\alttext(At a nonzero coefficient the residual correlation minus the quadratic gradient matches the signed L1 penalty. At zero it lies inside the L1 subgradient interval.)

`diagnostics()` reports the maximum violation of those conditions, objective and
coefficient change. Stopping requires max_change<=tol and violation<=tol times
max(1,max_j abs(Z_j^T u/n)). `max_iter` bounds full cyclic sweeps; a finite partial
fit is returned and flagged if that limit is reached. Full-data path and individual
fold results retain their own convergence evidence. This is a stationarity check,
not a duality-gap certificate or a prediction guarantee. One sweep costs O(n*p);
a length-L path with F folds costs O((F+1)*L*iterations*n*p), with warm starts.

>> Keep validation preprocessing local to each training fold

A finite negative `lambda` requests automatic CV. The common descending geometric
grid uses full-training correlations: lambda_max=max(abs(Z_j^T u/n))/alpha for
alpha>0, or a ridge-only heuristic using divisor .001. At zero correlation the maximum
falls back to one. The default minimum/maximum ratio is .01 when n<p and .0001 otherwise;
`lambda_min_ratio` can override it. Pure ridge has no finite all-zero cutoff. A search
boundary means the stated grid should be reconsidered, not that a global optimum was
found. Alpha is never tuned automatically by this API.

Balanced folds use a local SplitMix64 seed. Each fold computes its own training
predictor offsets/scales and response offset, fits the common lambdas, and predicts
its held-out rows with those frozen quantities. The candidate grid and Formula schema
are defined from the full training set, rather than separately per fold. CV pools
squared error over all held-out rows, weighting rows equally even for unequal folds:

$$
 \operatorname{CV}(\lambda)=\frac1n\sum_{f=1}^F\sum_{i\in V_f}
 \left(y_i-\widehat b_{0,-f}-x_i^T\widehat\beta_{-f}(\lambda)\right)^2.
$$
\alttext(Cross-validation averages held-out squared errors over rows, using coefficients and intercepts fitted without each row's fold.)

The first minimum on the descending path wins exact ties. The corresponding full-
training path fit supplies the final model. Candidates with finite but unfinished
optimization remain visible, including their status; no finite CV points are hidden
from the plot. The old report's divergent-fold workaround is removed: using a unit
coordinate denominator on arbitrary fold columns and reusing full-data preprocessing
were solver/pipeline errors, rather than reasons to conceal large errors.

Formula nulls drop rows before fitting; `kept_row_indices()` records the mapping.
Retained design/response values and arithmetic must be finite. Predictions use the
frozen Formula schema and raw-unit coefficients; query batches never fit new offsets.
A null-predictor query row receives NaN, while scoring omits null formula rows.
No p-values, confidence intervals or causal conclusions follow from selected slopes.

> Simple examples

>> Four rows with two orthogonal predictors

The columns x and z below are centred, orthogonal and have population squared norm
one. The response is exactly 3+2*x+.2*z, so rho=(2,.2). At lambda=.5, ridge gives
(4/3,2/15), lasso gives (1.5,0), and alpha=.5 gives (1.4,0). The intercept remains
three. This checks the signs, threshold, denominator and unpenalized intercept together.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/en_soft_threshold.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;
  d.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{-1, -1, 1, 1});
  d.add_column("z", std::vector<double>{-1, 1, -1, 1});
  d.add_column("y", std::vector<double>{.8, 1.2, 4.8, 5.2});
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const std::vector<dm::graphics::RGB> colors = {{37, 99, 235}, {22, 163, 74}, {220, 38, 38}};
  const std::vector<std::string>       labels = {"ridge", "elastic net", "lasso"};
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame               results;
  std::size_t                          index = 0;
  for (double alpha : {0., .5, 1.}) {
    std::vector<double> grid, bx, bz;
    for (int k = 0; k <= 40; ++k) {
      const double                lambda = k / 20.;
      const dm::stats::ElasticNet m(d, "y ~ x + z", {alpha, lambda});
      grid.push_back(lambda);
      bx.push_back(m.coefficients()[0]);
      bz.push_back(m.coefficients()[1]);
    }
    left.line(grid, bx, labels[index], colors[index]);
    right.line(grid, bz, labels[index], colors[index]);
    const dm::stats::ElasticNet m(d, "y ~ x + z", {alpha, .5});
    auto row = m.diagnostics().select({"alpha", "lambda", "objective", "max_kkt_violation"});
    row.add_column("x", std::vector<double>{m.coefficients()[0]});
    row.add_column("z", std::vector<double>{m.coefficients()[1]});
    row.add_column("intercept", std::vector<double>{m.intercept()});
    results = results.ncols() ? results.concat_rows(row) : row;
    ++index;
  }
  left.title("Strong coordinate: score 2")
      .x_label("lambda")
      .y_label("coefficient of x")
      .y_limits(0, 3.3);
  right.title("Weak coordinate: score .2")
      .x_label("lambda")
      .y_label("coefficient of z")
      .y_limits(0, .36);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/en_soft_threshold.svg");
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame::MarkdownOptions fmt;
  fmt.digits     = 7;
  fmt.scientific = {"max_kkt_violation"};
  std::print("{}\n\n{}\n", d.to_md(1), results.to_md(fmt));
}
```
