// result_end
\caption(The primary model’s path, full CV curve and numerical status use only the 120 training flowers.)
\alttext(Two plots show original-unit coefficient traces and every five-fold CV MSE. Tables expose training rows, raw and penalty coefficients, selected objective and KKT residual, and the lowest-error grid candidates. Counts report convergence across all path and fold fits.)

The primary grid selects lambda=.00237282 and has three nonzero slopes: the
interaction is zero at that penalty. All 60 full-data candidates and all 300 fold
fits converge. The selected KKT violation is about 6.5e-9; this is numerical evidence
about optimization, separate from the prediction checks below.

>> Compare declared alpha values and an unpenalized baseline

Use alpha=0,.25,.5,.75,1 as a declared sensitivity set. Each fit uses the same five
training folds and selects its own lambda over 60 candidates; the declared 1e-6 endpoint
ratio makes the ridge heuristic's low-penalty range accessible. Also fit lambda=0 with
the same formula and preprocessing as an unpenalized baseline. A reported nonzero count
uses the library threshold abs(original coefficient)>1e-10, not an effective-df formula.

Only now score the reserved test rows. The baseline RMSE uses the training response
mean, while held-out R-squared uses the test response mean in its denominator. This
single declared split provides an illustrative comparison, not uncertainty estimates
or a reason to choose alpha from test errors. The primary alpha remains .5. Prediction
uses the frozen training schema and coefficients, whether a flower is queried alone
or as part of the held-out batch.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/en_iris_test.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto               d = dm::datasets::iris();
  std::vector<std::size_t> train_rows, test_rows;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < d.nrows(); ++i)
    (i % 5 == 0 ? test_rows : train_rows).push_back(i);
  const auto        train = d.take_rows(train_rows), test = d.take_rows(test_rows);
  const std::string formula =
      "Petal.Length ~ Sepal.Length + Sepal.Width + Petal.Width + Sepal.Length:Petal.Width";
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame results;
  std::vector<double>    alpha_grid, cv_error, test_error;
  std::vector<double>    primary, ordinary;
  for (double alpha : {0., .25, .5, .75, 1.}) {
    dm::stats::ElasticNetOptions o;
    o.alpha            = alpha;
    o.n_lambda         = 60;
    o.max_iter         = 50000;
    o.tol              = 1e-8;
    o.lambda_min_ratio = 1e-6;
    const dm::stats::ElasticNet m(train, formula, o);
    auto                        row = m.score(test);
    row.add_column("alpha", std::vector<double>{alpha});
    row.add_column("lambda", std::vector<double>{m.lambda()});
    row.add_column("training_RMSE", std::vector<double>{m.rmse()});
    const double cv = std::sqrt(
        *std::min_element(m.cv_mean_squared_error().begin(), m.cv_mean_squared_error().end()));
    row.add_column("CV_RMSE", std::vector<double>{cv});
    row.add_column("nonzero", std::vector<double>{double(m.non_zero_coefficients())});
    row.add_column("converged", std::vector<double>{m.converged() ? 1. : 0.});
    row.add_column(
        "grid_boundary",
        std::vector<double>{
            m.lambda() == m.lambda_path().front() || m.lambda() == m.lambda_path().back() ? 1.
                                                                                          : 0.});
    results = results.ncols() ? results.concat_rows(row) : row;
    alpha_grid.push_back(alpha);
    cv_error.push_back(cv);
    test_error.push_back(row.double_at("rmse", 0));
    if (alpha == .5)
      primary = m.predict(test);
  }
  dm::stats::ElasticNetOptions o{.5, 0};
  o.max_iter = 50000;
  o.tol      = 1e-8;
  const dm::stats::ElasticNet baseline(train, formula, o);
  ordinary        = baseline.predict(test);
  auto       left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create(), right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  const auto actual = test.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values();
  left.points(actual, primary, "alpha .5", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
      .points(actual, ordinary, "lambda zero", {220, 38, 38}, 3)
      .line({.8, 7.5}, {.8, 7.5}, "identity", {148, 163, 184})
      .title("Predictions for 30 reserved flowers")
      .x_label("actual petal length (cm)")
      .y_label("predicted petal length (cm)")
      .x_limits(.8, 7.5)
      .y_limits(.8, 10);
  right.line(alpha_grid, cv_error, "training CV RMSE", {37, 99, 235})
      .points(alpha_grid, test_error, "test RMSE", {220, 38, 38}, 5)
      .title("Declared alpha sensitivity")
      .x_label("alpha")
      .y_label("prediction RMSE (cm)")
      .y_limits(0, .7);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2).add(left).add(right).size(1100, 490).fit_panels().save_svg(
      "build/en_iris_test.svg");
  std::print(
      "{}\n\nLambda-zero baseline (converged={}):\n\n{}\n",
      results
          .select(
              {"alpha",
               "lambda",
               "training_RMSE",
               "CV_RMSE",
               "rmse",
               "r_squared",
               "nonzero",
               "converged",
               "grid_boundary"})
          .to_md(7),
      baseline.converged(),
      baseline.score(test).to_md(7));
}
```
