\image(figures/en_iris_test.svg)
// result_end
\caption(CV tuning occurs on training flowers; reserved-row errors and a lambda-zero baseline are reported separately.)
\alttext(One panel compares actual held-out petal lengths with primary and unpenalized predictions. Another contrasts training CV RMSE and reserved test RMSE across declared alpha values. Tables identify selected lambdas, training and test metrics, nonzero counts, solver status and grid boundaries.)

For the primary alpha=.5 fit, CV RMSE is .3208 cm and reserved-row RMSE is .3504 cm.
The lambda-zero baseline has reserved-row RMSE .3403 cm, while predicting the training
mean gives 1.8162 cm. Regularization therefore does not improve test RMSE on this
split. Ridge selects the smallest lambda in its declared grid (.0016857), so its
reported choice is a boundary result, not evidence that a wider search could not
reduce CV error further. None of these single-split differences quantify uncertainty.

> Conclusion

The small examples connect the penalized objective to exact soft-thresholded coordinate
solutions, raw-unit coefficients and equal sharing of duplicate predictors under a
positive L2 contribution. Finite sweep budgets expose how numerical stationarity is
checked. Explicit fold refits confirm that validation predictions use training-only
preprocessing. Coefficient sparsity, coefficient uniqueness and predictive performance
remain separate properties.

The Iris comparison distinguishes training fit, CV selection and a reserved-row
prediction check. These measurements describe one formula, declared grids and one
split. They do not establish causal measurements, coefficient significance, reliable
variable selection across resamples or a universally optimal alpha/lambda. Search
boundaries and unfinished fits remain visible rather than being hidden from figures.

> References

- Zou, H. and Hastie, T. (2005). [Regularization and variable selection via the elastic net|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9868.2005.00503.x]. Journal of the Royal Statistical Society B 67(2), 301–320. Original motivation/grouping; this report uses the direct objective, without the paper’s extra rescaling.
- Friedman, J., Hastie, T. and Tibshirani, R. (2010). [Regularization Paths for Generalized Linear Models via Coordinate Descent|https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v033.i01]. Journal of Statistical Software 33(1), 1–22. Objective and cyclic coordinate updates.
- scikit-learn developers. [ElasticNet|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.linear_model.ElasticNet.html]. Objective parameter correspondence and independent solver comparisons with explicitly matched preprocessing.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. Annals of Eugenics 7, 179–188. Iris context.

